Explicit uniform-buffer lifetime and storage ownership
======================================================

Source: docs/Rendering.UniformBufferLifetime.Proposal.md.
Parent: docs/PBR.WaterPerformance.todo / Phase 17.
Review status: Phases 1–6 complete; final second review and completion audit passed on 2026-10-05.
Contracts: docs/ShaderAuthoring.md; docs/GPU.ShaderDemandLoading.md;
  docs/GPU.UniformContractValidation.md; docs/Rendering.AuthoritativePipelineState.Proposal.md.
Evidence belongs in docs/Rendering.UniformBufferLifetime.md.

Execution contract
  Preserve std140 packing, generated validation-before-publication, mutation guards, numeric slots,
  graphics/compute activation, explicit sharing, reload and resource/cache ownership.
  Lifetime is fixed per logical instance, independent of write frequency and shader variants.
  No global byte deduplication, new shader registry, engine draw-count hook or unrelated renames.
  Delegate builds/tests. Never launch the game; distinguish counters from CPU/GPU/live timings.
  Review twice and audit completion before checking tasks. Retained compatibility must be explicit.

Phase 1: Inventory and storage decisions
  ☑ Inventory CPU blocks, writes, sizes, sharing and disposal; choose water and non-water consumers.
  ☑ Establish mapped/conventional pool policy, capacity/overflow, alignment, context and frame owner.
  ☑ Record reference transient allocations/copies, binds, resident bytes, retirement and fence waits.
  Gate: allocation/lifecycle choices follow actual consumers; all linked contracts are reconciled.

Phase 2: Logical publication and transient compatibility
  Dependencies: Phase 1.
  ☑ Add fixed SingleDraw/SingleFrame/MultiFrame usage and separate logical/physical publication owners.
  ☑ Keep CpuUniformBuffer packing/revisions; common named/prepared publication commits only on success.
  ☑ Preserve SingleFrame compatibility default, multiple same-frame updates, full copies, guards,
    repeated BeginFrame epochs, EndFrame closure, ring exhaustion and mapped/conventional paths.
  ☑ SingleDraw gets a new range per independent publication; retain current liquid draw usage until
    an owned draw/dispatch boundary proves that stronger promise. Restoration does not count draws.
  ☑ Failed publication retains previous successful version/dirty data; release unpublished reservations.
  ☑ Keep legacy CpuUniformBuffer.Dispose reset behavior explicit; logical resource disposal is terminal.
  Gate: existing same-frame savings and failure/retry semantics survive both publication entry points.

Phase 3: Persistent storage and last-use retirement
  Dependencies: Phase 2.
  ☑ Add pooled aligned retained ranges beside the ring, under the same frame/context owner.
  ☑ Support persistent coherent/explicit-flush mappings and conventional uploads without orphaning live data.
  ☑ Track every bind including unchanged versions and scope restoration; group retirement behind fences.
  ☑ Reclaim released versions only after latest use completes; never recycle a current owned version.
  ☑ Reject stale allocator/page generations; handle overflow, failure and renderer-owned teardown.
  ☑ Count allocations, payload copies, binds, resident/pending bytes and waits independently.
  Gate: immutable snapshots and cross-frame reuse work with both backends; elapsed frames never prove safety.

Phase 4: Consumer ownership and adoption
  Dependencies: Phase 3.
  ✔ Assign verified persistent candidates from the water/non-water inventory; retain frequently changed @done(26-10-05 14:14)
    data as SingleFrame rather than inventing persistent consumers without stable contents.
  ✔ Keep independent surface/volume inputs and explicit shared instance ownership. @done(26-10-05 14:14)
  ✔ Preserve shader reload/slot changes; reject incompatible layouts, retain compatible CPU contents on executable reload. @done(26-10-05 14:14)
  ✔ Release owned versions with their owner; borrowing shader disposal must not retire shared data. @done(26-10-05 14:14)
  ✔ Remove duplicated upload caches after named/prepared paths share authority; retain raw GPU paths. @done(26-10-05 14:14)
  Gate: consumer write/lifetime table identifies every migrated and compatibility family with reasons.

Phase 5: Behavioral verification and measurements
  Dependencies: Phases 2–4.
  ✔ Delegate Debug/Release builds with normal shader receipts and focused unit/headless suites. @done(26-10-05 14:17)
  ✔ Observe actual GPU snapshots across draws/dispatches, same-frame edits, nested restoration, sharing, @done(26-10-05 14:17)
    different slots, reload, incompatible layouts and disposal.
  ✔ Verify zero unchanged cross-frame copies, transient rollover/repeated indices, pool reuse, @done(26-10-05 14:17)
    delayed completion and replacement after an old version was rebound in a later frame.
  ✔ Exercise failed allocation/upload/bind and retry, mapping/flush/fallback, alignment and overflow. @done(26-10-05 14:17)
  ✔ Compare matched changed/per-frame/stable workloads, including water and non-water consumers; @done(26-10-05 14:17)
    report allocations/copies/binds, resident storage, pending retirement and waits separately.
  Gate: no source-text substitute for behavior; CPU/GPU/live costs explicitly unmeasured unless measured.

Phase 6: Documentation, second review and completion audit
  Dependencies: Phase 5.
  ☑ Update authoring, proposal, evidence and parent links with usage/sharing/disposal/reload contracts.
  ☑ Map every proposal section to source, tests and measured results; identify compatibility paths.
  ☑ Perform second source review and independent audit against the full proposal and linked contracts.
  ☑ Resolve all lifetime/retirement findings; optional later consumer tuning does not defer correctness.
  Gate: all required work and validation complete; parent Phase 17 may then be marked complete.

Proposal mapping
  Intent/current implementation -> Phases 1, 5; lifetime/logical identity/publication -> Phase 2.
  Transient storage -> Phase 2; persistent storage/retirement/OpenGL -> Phase 3.
  Sharing/reload/disposal -> Phases 2–4; integration/source layout -> Phases 2–4, 6.
  Consumer migration -> Phases 1, 4; verification/measurements/completion -> Phases 5–6.

Completion evidence: docs/Rendering.UniformBufferLifetime.md; Debug and Release 80/80 each,
  zero skips. Audit added four direct-write/MarkDirty and transient-exhaustion cases.
  Context replacement remains explicitly excluded; CPU/GPU/live timing is unmeasured.
